**Setup:** `Order` with `@OneToMany List<OrderItem> items`. Loading 3 orders, each with 5 items.

**Strategy 1 — SELECT (default LAZY) — 4 queries (N+1):**

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
List<OrderItem> items;
```
```sql
SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 3;
SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = 1;
SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = 2;
SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = 3;
```

**Strategy 2 — JOIN FETCH — 1 query but breaks pagination:**

```java
@Query("select distinct o from Order o join fetch o.items")
List<Order> findAllWithItems();
```
- One query: `SELECT o.*, oi.* FROM orders o LEFT JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.id`.
- With `Pageable` -> `HHH90003004` warning, paginates in Java.

**Strategy 3 — SUBSELECT — 2 queries, pagination-safe:**

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@Fetch(FetchMode.SUBSELECT)
List<OrderItem> items;
```
```sql
SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 3;
SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id IN (SELECT id FROM orders LIMIT 3);
```

**Strategy 4 — `@BatchSize` — `ceil(N/batch) + 1` queries, pagination-safe:**

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@BatchSize(size = 50)
List<OrderItem> items;
```
```sql
SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 3;
SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id IN (1, 2, 3);
```

**Global default in `application.yml`:**

```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size: 50
```

- One line that fixes 90% of N+1 bugs.
- Applies `@BatchSize(50)` to every association without an override.

**Canonical bidirectional `@OneToMany`:**

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order",
               cascade = {CascadeType.PERSIST, CascadeType.MERGE},
               orphanRemoval = true)
    private final Set<OrderItem> items = new HashSet<>();

    public Set<OrderItem> getItems() { return Collections.unmodifiableSet(items); }

    public void addItem(OrderItem item) {
        items.add(item);
        item.setOrder(this);
    }

    public void removeItem(OrderItem item) {
        items.remove(item);
        item.setOrder(null);
    }
}

@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @Id Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
    @JoinColumn(name = "order_id")
    private Order order;

    void setOrder(Order order) { this.order = order; }
}
```

**Why this is the gold pattern:**
- `Collections.unmodifiableSet` prevents direct mutation.
- Package-private `setOrder` ensures both sides stay in sync.
- `orphanRemoval = true` deletes items removed from the collection.
- `cascade = {PERSIST, MERGE}` propagates saves; `REMOVE` is handled by `orphanRemoval`.

**`@ManyToMany` with metadata — junction entity:**

```java
@Entity
public class Enrollment {
    @EmbeddedId EnrollmentId id;
    @ManyToOne @MapsId("studentId") Student student;
    @ManyToOne @MapsId("courseId")  Course  course;
    Instant enrolledAt;
    Grade   grade;
}

@Entity
public class Student {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "student", cascade = ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    Set<Enrollment> enrollments;
}
```

- **`@ManyToMany` is rarely the right model** — relationships almost always accrue metadata.
- Modeling the junction as an entity from day one avoids costly schema migrations later.

**Pattern 1 — Parent owns children (line items, addresses):**

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id Long id;
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order",
               cascade = {CascadeType.PERSIST, CascadeType.MERGE},
               orphanRemoval = true)
    Set<OrderItem> items;
}

orderRepo.save(order);             // saves order + new items
order.getItems().remove(item);     // DELETE FROM order_items WHERE id=?
orderRepo.save(order);
orderRepo.delete(order);           // DELETE items, then DELETE order
```

**Pattern 2 — Parent references shared children (tags, categories):**

```java
@Entity
public class Article {
    @ManyToMany
    Set<Tag> tags;
}

tagRepo.save(tag);
article.getTags().add(tag);
articleRepo.save(article);         // INSERT junction row only
article.getTags().remove(tag);
articleRepo.save(article);         // DELETE junction row only
articleRepo.delete(article);       // DELETE junction rows + article (tags untouched)
```

**Pattern 3 — `@ManyToMany` with metadata (junction entity):**

```java
@Entity
public class Enrollment {
    @EmbeddedId EnrollmentId id;
    @ManyToOne(fetch = LAZY) @MapsId("studentId") Student student;
    @ManyToOne(fetch = LAZY) @MapsId("courseId")  Course  course;
    Instant enrolledAt;
}

@Entity
public class Student {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "student",
               cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
               orphanRemoval = true)
    Set<Enrollment> enrollments;
}
```

- **`CascadeType.ALL` is safe** here because the junction entity is owned by the student.
- Deleting the student deletes enrollments but **not** the courses themselves.
